84% of women are “not satisfied emotionally with their relationships” (p. 804);
70% of all women “married five or more years are having sex outside of their marriages” (p. 856);
95% of women “report forms of emotional and psychological harassment from men with whom they are in love relationships” (p. 810);
84% of women report forms of condescension from the men in their love relationships (p. 809).



The sample was self-selected. The recipients of the questionnaire decided whether they would be in the sample. Hite mailed 100,000 questionnaires and only 4.5% were returned.
The questionnaires went to particular groups, such as professional women’s groups, counseling centers, church societies and senior citizens’ centers, whose members may hold views different from those of other women in the United States.
The questionnaire was very long. It had 127 essay questions, most with several parts. Who will tend to return such a survey? Probably women with strong feelings about the subject, who may differ systematically from those who did not reply.
Many questions were vague (what counts as “love”?), which makes the percentages hard to interpret.
Many questions were leading, suggesting to the respondent which answer to give.

A.2 Simple Random Sampling
Notation: \(\hat p\) is the sample proportion of a 0/1 variable; \(e\) is the margin of error.
Mean (Section 3.2): \[
\boxed{
\hat{V}(\bar{y}) = \left(1 - \frac{n}{N}\right)\frac{s^2}{n}, \qquad
\mathrm{SE}(\bar y) = \sqrt{\Big(1-\frac nN\Big)\frac{s^2}{n}}}
\]
Total (Section 3.2): \[
\boxed{
\hat{t} = N\bar{y}, \qquad
\hat{V}(\hat{t}) = N^2\left(1 - \frac{n}{N}\right)\frac{s^2}{n}, \qquad
\mathrm{SE}(\hat{t}) = N\,\mathrm{SE}(\bar{y})}
\]
Proportion (Section 3.2); the number of units with the characteristic is estimated by \(N\hat p\) with SE \(N\,\mathrm{SE}(\hat p)\): \[
\boxed{\hat V(\hat p) = \Big(1-\frac nN\Big)\frac{\hat p (1-\hat p)}{n-1}}
\]
Sample size for margin of error \(e\) at level \(1-\alpha\), with \(n_0 = (z_{\alpha/2}S/e)^2\) (Section 3.5): \[
\boxed{n = \frac{n_0}{1+n_0/N} = \frac{z_{\alpha/2}^2 S^2}{e^2 + z_{\alpha/2}^2 S^2/N}}
\]

A.3 Stratified Random Sampling
Notation: stratum \(h = 1,\ldots,H\) has \(N_h\) units, of which \(n_h\) are sampled by SRS; \(\pi_h = N_h/N\) is the stratum weight; \(\overline y_h\), \(s_h^2\) and \(\hat p_h\) are the sample mean, variance and proportion in stratum \(h\); \(S_h^2\) is the population variance in stratum \(h\); \(c_h\) is the cost of sampling one unit in stratum \(h\).
Estimators of the total and the mean (Section 4.2): \[
\boxed{
\hat t_{\text{str}} = \sum_{h=1}^H N_h \overline y_h,
\qquad
\overline y_{\text{str}} = \frac{\hat t_{\text{str}}}{N} = \sum_{h=1}^H \pi_h\overline y_h}
\]
Variance and SE (\(v_h\) is the contribution of stratum \(h\); \(\hat V(\hat t_{\text{str}}) = N^2\hat V(\overline y_{\text{str}})\)) (Section 4.2): \[
\boxed{
\hat V(\overline y_{\text{str}}) = \sum_{h=1}^H v_h, \qquad
v_h = \Big(1-\frac{n_h}{N_h}\Big)\pi_h^2\frac{s_h^2}{n_h}, \qquad
\mathrm{SE}(\overline y_{\text{str}}) = \sqrt{\hat V(\overline y_{\text{str}})}}
\]
Proportion (Section 4.4): \[
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
\hat p_{\text{str}} &= \sum_{h=1}^H \frac{N_h}{N}\hat p_h, \\
\hat V(\hat p_{\text{str}}) &= \sum_{h=1}^H \Big(1-\frac{n_h}{N_h}\Big)\Big(\frac{N_h}{N}\Big)^2\frac{\hat p_h(1-\hat p_h)}{n_h-1}
\end{aligned}}
\]
Proportional allocation \(n_h = n\pi_h\) (Section 4.7): \[
\boxed{V_{\text{prop}}(\overline y_{\text{str}}) = \left(1 - \frac{n}{N}\right)\frac{1}{n}\sum_{h=1}^H \pi_h S_h^2}
\]
Gain over SRS under proportional allocation; SSB and SSTO are the between-strata and total sums of squares of the population (Section 4.7): \[
\boxed{\text{percentage of variance reduction} \approx \frac{\text{SSB}}{\text{SSTO}} = R^2_{y\mid\text{strata}}}
\]
Optimal allocation for a fixed cost (Section 4.8): \[
\boxed{
n_h = \left(\frac{N_hS_h/\sqrt{c_h}}{\sum_{l=1}^H N_lS_l/\sqrt{c_l}}\right)n,
\qquad n_h \propto \frac{N_hS_h}{\sqrt{c_h}}}
\]
Neyman allocation (equal costs \(c_h\)) and its variance (Section 4.8): \[
\boxed{n_h = n \frac{N_h S_h}{\sum_{l=1}^H N_l S_l}}, \qquad
\boxed{V_{\text{opt}}(\overline{y}_{\text{str}}) = \frac{1}{n}\left(\sum_{h=1}^H \pi_h S_h\right)^2 - \frac{1}{N}\sum_{h=1}^H \pi_h S_h^2}
\]

A.5 Cluster Sampling
Notation: an SRS \(\mathcal S\) of \(n\) of the \(N\) clusters (psus); cluster \(i\) has \(M_i\) elements (ssus), total \(t_i\) and mean \(\bar y_i\); \(M_0 = \sum_{i=1}^N M_i\); \(\bar M = \frac1n\sum_{i\in\mathcal S}M_i\) is the average size of the sampled clusters; \(s_t^2\) is the sample variance of the \(t_i\). In two-stage sampling \(m_i\) of the \(M_i\) elements are subsampled, with mean \(\bar y_i\) and variance \(s_i^2\), and \(\hat t_i = M_i\bar y_i\).
One stage, equal sizes \(M_i = M\): an SRS of cluster totals (Section 6.3): \[
\boxed{\hat t = \frac Nn\sum_{i\in\mathcal S} t_i, \qquad
\mathrm{SE}(\hat t) = N\sqrt{\Big(1-\frac nN\Big)\frac{s_t^2}{n}}, \qquad
\hat{\bar y} = \frac{\hat t}{NM}}
\]
One stage, unequal sizes: ratio estimator of the mean per element (Section 6.5): \[
\boxed{
\hat{\bar y}_r = \frac{\sum_{i\in\mathcal S} t_i}{\sum_{i\in\mathcal S} M_i} = \frac{\sum_{i\in\mathcal S} M_i\bar y_i}{\sum_{i\in\mathcal S} M_i}}
\] \[
\boxed{
\mathrm{SE}(\hat{\bar y}_r) = \sqrt{\Big(1-\frac nN\Big)\frac{s_e^2}{n\bar M^2}}, \qquad
s_e^2 = \frac{1}{n-1}\sum_{i\in\mathcal S}e_i^2, \qquad e_i = t_i-\hat{\bar y}_r M_i}
\]
Two stages: ratio estimator (Section 6.6): \[
\boxed{
\hat{\bar y}_r = \frac{\sum_{i\in\mathcal S}\hat t_i}{\sum_{i\in\mathcal S}M_i} = \frac{\sum_{i\in\mathcal S}M_i\bar y_i}{\sum_{i\in\mathcal S}M_i}}
\]
Two stages: variance, a ratio piece plus \(n/N\) times a stratified piece, with \(e_i = \hat t_i - \hat{\bar y}_r M_i\) and \(M = n\bar M\) (Section 6.6): \[
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
\hat V(\hat{\bar y}_r) &= \hat V_{\text{ratio}} + \frac nN\,\hat V_{\text{str}}, \\
\hat V_{\text{ratio}} &= \Big(1-\frac nN\Big)\frac{s_e^2}{n\bar M^2}, \qquad
\hat V_{\text{str}} = \sum_{i\in\mathcal S}\Big(\frac{M_i}{M}\Big)^2\Big(1-\frac{m_i}{M_i}\Big)\frac{s_i^2}{m_i}
\end{aligned}}
\]
